      {
        "id": "p22-q14-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the values of $a$, $b$ and $c$ so that $3x^2-5x+7=a(x-1)^2+b(x-1)+c$ for all $x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=3$, $b=1$, $c=5$.",
          "worked": "Equating coefficients of $x^2$ gives $a=3$.\n\nSubstitute $x=1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}c&=3(1)^2-5(1)+7\\\\c&=5\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute $x=0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}7&=3-b+5\\\\b&=1\\end{align*}$$\nThus $a=3$, $b=1$ and $c=5$, so $3x^2-5x+7=3(x-1)^2+(x-1)+5$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence evaluate $3x^2-5x+7$ when $x=1.12$ without using a calculator.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$5.1632$.",
          "worked": "Using the identity from part a, $x-1=0.12$:\n$$\\begin{align*}3x^2-5x+7&=3(0.12)^2+0.12+5\\\\3x^2-5x+7&=3(0.0144)+0.12+5\\\\3x^2-5x+7&=0.0432+0.12+5\\\\3x^2-5x+7&=5.1632\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      }
    ]
  },
